<FieldGuide title="Drying and storing specimens" description="How to dry and store fungal specimens so their DNA survives: temperature and time guides, keeping collections apart, storage, and keeping a fungarium at home." eyebrow="FIELD RESEARCH TOOLKIT / DRYING & STORAGE" heading="Dry it right, <em>or the DNA is gone.</em>" lead="A specimen you can sequence in five years starts in the dehydrator tonight. Here’s what the protocols say, and why." tourKey="field-drying" tour={tour} sources={sources} current="drying">

<section id="why">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">Why it matters</p>
  <h2>The dried specimen is the evidence.</h2>
  <p>Once a mushroom is dried and labelled, it becomes a voucher: the physical record someone can go back to, re-measure, re-sequence or name. A photo can’t be sequenced. A rotten or overheated specimen often can’t either.</p>
  <p>Two things work against you. <strong>Heat</strong> breaks DNA down, so drying too hot ruins a specimen even though it looks fine.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup> <strong>Moisture</strong> lets the specimen rot or mould while it dries, and goes on damaging DNA after it’s dry if it’s stored damp.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup> Start drying as soon as you can after collecting.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup></p>
</section>

<section id="temperature">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">The temperature</p>
  <h2>Warm, not hot. Use a dehydrator with a thermostat.</h2>
  <div class="mv-guide-grid">
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>110–120 °F (43–50 °C)</h3><p>The FunDiS recommendation, for a dehydrator with temperature control.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup></p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Never above 155 °F (68 °C)</h3><p>FunDiS warns that temperatures above this can damage DNA.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup> Many food dehydrators go higher than that, so check the dial.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Lower is gentler</h3><p>Other protocols go lower: 40–45 °C in a Queensland manual,<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">4</a></sup> and 30 °C for 48 hours in a 2022 study of DNA quality, which also cites 35 °C for 48 hours.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">5</a></sup> Lower settings take longer, so the dehydrator is full for longer.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="mv-guide-note"><p><strong>Where the sources differ:</strong> they agree that high heat is the risk. They don’t agree on one best setting, so “low and steady, and never hot” is the safe reading. A 2017 comparison of drying methods for mushroom DNA is listed below if you want the detail.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">6</a></sup></p></div>
  <div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: the dehydrator, with the thermostat set</div>
</section>

<section id="when-dry">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">When it’s done</p>
  <h2>“Cracker dry”: it snaps, it doesn’t bend.</h2>
  <p>FunDiS describes a finished specimen as cracker dry, or as dry as potato chips. That usually takes 12 to 48 hours, depending on how big and how wet the mushroom was.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup><sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">2</a></sup> A thick bolete stem takes far longer than a small <em>Mycena</em>, so check each collection rather than the whole rack at once.</p>
  <div class="mv-guide-do">
    <div><h3>Done</h3><ul><li>Snaps cleanly when bent</li><li>Feels light, with no cool or soft spots</li><li>The thickest part (stem base, bulb, cap centre) is dry too</li></ul></div>
    <div><h3>Not yet</h3><ul><li>Bends or feels leathery</li><li>The centre of the stem or cap is still soft</li><li>Feels heavier than it looks</li></ul></div>
  </div>
  <p>If you slice a large specimen to help it dry, keep every piece together with the same number.</p>
</section>

<section id="times">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">Starting points</p>
  <h2>Time and temperature: a guide, not a rule.</h2>
  <p>Every collection dries differently. Size, weight, how wet it was and the species all change how long it takes, so use these as a place to start and go by the snap test, not the clock.</p>
  <div style="overflow:auto"><table class="mv-guide-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Collection</th><th>Setting</th><th>Expect</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Small and thin</strong><br><span>e.g. <em>Mycena</em>, <em>Marasmius</em>, small brown gilled mushrooms</span></td><td>110–120 °F (43–50 °C)</td><td>The short end of the 12–48 hours.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup> Check early; they can be done overnight.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Medium gilled mushrooms</strong><br><span>e.g. <em>Russula</em>, <em>Cortinarius</em>, most mid-sized caps</span></td><td>110–120 °F (43–50 °C)</td><td>Somewhere in the middle. Thick stems and cap centres are the last parts to dry.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Large, fleshy or wet</strong><br><span>e.g. boletes, big <em>Amanita</em>, thick-stemmed or waterlogged collections</span></td><td>110–120 °F (43–50 °C)</td><td>The long end, and sometimes longer. <strong>Boletes in particular tend to take a long time.</strong> Slicing them helps; keep every piece with its number.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="muted" style="font-size:.9rem">The setting comes from FunDiS;<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup> the time ranges by type are from experience, not from a study.</p>
  <div class="mv-guide-note"><p><strong>No thermostat?</strong> Many cheap dehydrators have only an on/off switch, so you can’t set the temperature or know what it is. Put an oven or probe thermometer on a tray for one run to see what yours actually reaches. If it goes above 155 °F (68 °C), it’s not suitable for specimens you want to sequence.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup></p></div>
</section>

<section id="rack">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">On the rack</p>
  <h2>The dehydrator is where collections get mixed up.</h2>
  <p>FunDiS advises keeping contact between species to a minimum and labelling specimens on the rack.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup> Specimens that touch can pass tissue and spores to each other, and a single swapped label undoes everything else you did right.</p>
  <ol class="mv-guide-steps">
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Loading</span><p>Put each collection in its own spot, with space around it, and its number (the corner of its field slip, or a printed label) right beside it. Another way, which I use: lay collections out in the order you made the observations, starting from a fixed point (12 o’clock on a round dehydrator), so position gives the order.</p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Before it goes in</span><p>Photograph each tray. Some mushrooms dry down to look very different, and the photo lets you match them up in the morning.</p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Mixed sizes</span><p>Put the slow, thick collections where you can reach them without disturbing the rest, because they’ll stay on after the others come off.</p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Unloading</span><p>Bag one collection at a time, with its label, before you touch the next. Never take several off the rack and sort them afterwards.</p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">If in doubt</span><p>If you can’t confidently say which collection a dried piece belongs to, discard it, or make a new observation of it and note what happened.</p></li>
  </ol>
  <div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: a loaded rack, with each collection’s number beside it</div>
  <p>More on numbering and keeping things matched: <a href="/investigate/field/tracking/">Specimen tracking</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="storage">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">Storage</p>
  <h2>Keep it dry once it’s dry.</h2>
  <p>Dried fungi absorb moisture from the air, and that moisture degrades DNA. FunDiS recommends adding desiccant if the relative humidity where you store specimens is above 50%.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup></p>
  <div class="mv-guide-grid">
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Seal it</h3><p>A sealed bag for each collection, with its label inside the bag, not only written on the outside.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Know your humidity</h3><p>An inexpensive hygrometer tells you whether your storage space is above 50%. If it is, add desiccant (silica gel) to the bags or the box.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup><sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">5</a></sup></p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Store it where you can find it</h3><p>Pick one grouping and stick to it: by month, by foray event, or by sequencing plate. Storing by plate means any sequencing result leads you straight back to the specimen. The <a href="/investigate/plate-tagger/">Plate Tagger</a> writes the plate onto each iNaturalist observation.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="fungarium">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">A fungarium at home</p>
  <h2>A collection you can actually use.</h2>
  <p>A home fungarium is just your dried specimens, kept so that any one of them can be found from its record, and its record found from it.</p>
  <div class="mv-guide-grid">
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Findable</h3><p>Pick one way to group the bags, by month, foray event or sequencing plate, and note it on the observation (a voucher field, or the plate via the <a href="/investigate/plate-tagger/">Plate Tagger</a>). The test: given an observation, can you put your hand on the bag in a minute?</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Dry</h3><p>Sealed bags, label inside, desiccant if the room is above 50% humidity.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup> Boxes or bins keep the bags flat and out of the light.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Honest</h3><p>The record should say what you actually have. If a specimen is thrown away, update its voucher field so nobody goes looking for it later. The <a href="/investigate/collection-check/">Collection Check</a> shows where records and specimens may have drifted apart.</p></div>
  </div>
  <h3 style="margin-top:20px">What to keep</h3>
  <p>A home fungarium can’t keep everything forever. How Geeky decides:</p>
  <div class="mv-guide-do">
    <div><h3>Keep</h3><ul><li>Every provisional species</li><li>Rarely collected species</li><li>Anything not yet confidently identified</li></ul></div>
    <div><h3>Discard, once identified</h3><ul><li>Known, described, common species, once the identification is settled (for example, by its sequence)</li></ul></div>
  </div>
  <p>When you discard one, note it on the observation (for example, in the voucher field), so the record doesn’t point to a specimen that’s gone.</p>
  <p><strong>Discarding a specimen is about shelf space, not about the species.</strong> Keep observing common species: people tend to photograph what’s unusual, so common species are often under-recorded, and they’re key to seeing how nature is changing.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">7</a></sup></p>
  <p>Not sure whether something is rare? Step 4 of the <a href="/investigate/collection-check/">Collection Check</a> ranks each of your species by how often others have recorded and sequenced it in your state, and the <a href="/investigate/provisional-finder/">Provisional Finder</a> shows how often a provisional has been collected and sequenced, and who else has it. If a specimen might become the basis of a new species description, talk to a public herbarium about depositing it rather than keeping it only at home.</p>
</section>

<section id="routine">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">How Geeky does it</p>
  <h2>My setup</h2>
  <p>I use two dehydrators. One is an inexpensive round, stacking one with no temperature control; there are many very similar versions, and it’s a cheap way to start. The other has a thermostat. If you’re buying one for specimens you want sequenced, get one with a thermostat if you can, or check what a basic one actually reaches before you trust it (see “No thermostat?” above).</p>
  <div class="mv-guide-ext"><a href="https://a.co/d/06su6Qnr">The round dehydrator I use ↗<small>Amazon · no temperature control · many similar versions</small></a></div>
  <div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: the dehydrators</div>
  <p>Collections go on in observation order, starting at 12 o’clock, and I photograph every tray. They come off and get bagged the next morning, in about 30 minutes, and the bags are grouped by month, by sequencing plate, or by foray event.</p>
  <p>Planning how many collections your dehydrator can handle in a week? Try the <a href="/investigate/collection-planner/">Collection Planner</a>.</p>
</section>

</FieldGuide>
